--TEST--
`Core\Fatal::onLimit` registers a handler, replaces it when called again, and runs neither — `rule:errors/on-limit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:errors/on-limit`'s tier 1 is a *registration*, and the thing worth pinning about
// a registration is the boundary it does not cross: nothing about handing the
// callable over is allowed to call it. A ladder that ran the handler eagerly —
// or one that ran it to "check" it, which is the shape zero retries exists to
// forbid — prints a line here that the expectation does not have. That is the
// edge this case asks about; what a breach then does with the handler is the
// slice that reaches it.
Core\Fatal::onLimit(fn (): void => {
    echo "first ran\n";
});
echo "registered\n";

// § 1 gives the tier one handler and not a chain, so a second registration
// replaces the first rather than queueing behind it — asserted here by the
// same silence, because a queue and a replacement are only told apart when the
// ladder fires and neither may fire now.
Core\Fatal::onLimit(fn (): void => {
    echo "second ran\n";
});
echo "replaced\n";

// A handler is a callable like any other, so registering one neither consumes it
// nor makes it unusable: the value stays a `callable` the program still holds.
// Calling it directly is a plain call, and it is the one place this case may
// print from the callable at all.
var $handler = fn (): void => { echo "called directly\n"; };
Core\Fatal::onLimit($handler);
$handler();
echo "done\n";
--EXPECT--
registered
replaced
called directly
done
